WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.921 --> 00:00:13.384
In 1315, Scotland’s King Robert the Bruce
decided to lay siege

00:00:13.384 --> 00:00:15.428
to England's Carlisle Castle.

00:00:15.803 --> 00:00:18.014
Robert expected a swift victory,

00:00:18.014 --> 00:00:22.101
but his men arrived to find
a relentless downpour.

00:00:22.685 --> 00:00:24.979
Struggling against muck and mud,

00:00:25.104 --> 00:00:27.940
they tried to fill the moat
with corn husks

00:00:28.066 --> 00:00:30.443
only for the husks to float away.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.697
Then Robert ordered the construction
of a massive siege tower,

00:00:34.906 --> 00:00:37.992
but the English responded
with wooden scaffolds

00:00:37.992 --> 00:00:40.161
that heightened the castle walls.

00:00:40.828 --> 00:00:45.833
Their final push to scale those walls
ended with English arrows raining down

00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:47.710
on the sodden Scots.

00:00:48.086 --> 00:00:51.631
At a time when sieges could last
months or even years,

00:00:51.631 --> 00:00:55.009
Robert retreated after just 11 days.

00:00:55.551 --> 00:00:59.430
This siege is a prime example
of how medieval European castles

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.433
could stand firm against enemy onslaughts.

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:05.937
But castles weren’t just
for passive defense;

00:01:06.145 --> 00:01:09.440
they were symbols of power,
private residences,

00:01:09.607 --> 00:01:14.821
and hubs of governance intended
to impress and intimidate.

00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:20.326
In fact, they were just as likely
to be used for offensive military tactics.

00:01:20.743 --> 00:01:24.455
Castles regularly served
as bases for launching attacks

00:01:24.455 --> 00:01:28.334
and as a way to control local populations
with promises of shelter

00:01:28.584 --> 00:01:30.837
or threats of violence.

00:01:31.546 --> 00:01:37.760
While citadels and fortified palaces could
be found as early as the 8th century BCE,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.012
European castles as we think of them

00:01:40.012 --> 00:01:44.725
started appearing in large numbers
in the late 9th century CE.

00:01:44.976 --> 00:01:48.729
At this time, the ruling
Carolingian Empire was breaking down.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.398
And as the monarch’s control

00:01:50.398 --> 00:01:54.026
over his 1 million square kilometer
realm disintegrated,

00:01:54.152 --> 00:01:58.656
nobles began building castles
to solidify their regional power.

00:01:59.031 --> 00:02:03.452
These early wooden castles
followed the motte and bailey design,

00:02:03.703 --> 00:02:06.914
featuring a central tower
built atop a steep mound

00:02:06.914 --> 00:02:09.041
with a walled yard at the base.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.712
But over the following centuries,
as castles became centers

00:02:12.712 --> 00:02:15.923
of social, cultural, military,
and political life,

00:02:16.299 --> 00:02:19.594
their designs grew more varied
and complex.

00:02:19.969 --> 00:02:25.308
By the 12th century, many wooden motte
and baileys had been rebuilt with stone,

00:02:25.391 --> 00:02:28.019
increasing their defense and longevity.

00:02:28.644 --> 00:02:31.564
Subsequent castles built in stone
from the start

00:02:31.564 --> 00:02:36.402
used curtain walls and gatehouses
to enclose the castle yard.

00:02:36.777 --> 00:02:41.866
And their walls and towers were often
coated in a layer of protective limewash,

00:02:41.991 --> 00:02:46.454
covering their stony grey
with a range of colorful tones.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.998
Alongside their scale,

00:02:49.207 --> 00:02:52.919
visual beauty was a way
for castles to project power.

00:02:53.502 --> 00:02:55.922
Castle Caernarfon in northern Wales

00:02:55.922 --> 00:02:58.883
was built with bands of
different colored stone.

00:02:59.175 --> 00:03:02.220
And Crusader armies building castles
in the Middle East

00:03:02.345 --> 00:03:07.016
often incorporated elements of intricate
Islamic and Byzantine architecture.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.645
But as European castles
became more widespread,

00:03:10.811 --> 00:03:12.939
so did technologies to defeat them.

00:03:13.105 --> 00:03:16.150
Fire had long threatened
wooden motte and baileys,

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:19.028
and while their stone counterparts
were more resistant,

00:03:19.028 --> 00:03:22.657
their underlying wooden structure
was still vulnerable to arson.

00:03:23.199 --> 00:03:25.993
Battering rams cracked castle doors,

00:03:25.993 --> 00:03:30.081
siege towers and mining operations
thwarted castle walls,

00:03:30.081 --> 00:03:34.335
and trebuchets launching stones
up to 130 kilograms

00:03:34.335 --> 00:03:36.587
endangered entire structures.

00:03:37.338 --> 00:03:40.216
Rectangular castle towers
were especially vulnerable

00:03:40.216 --> 00:03:44.011
since their square corners
had fewer load bearing stones.

00:03:44.845 --> 00:03:48.599
Fortunately, when the Crusaders
returned to Europe from the Middle East,

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:51.560
they brought home
architectural innovations,

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.106
including the design
for concentric castles.

00:03:55.273 --> 00:03:59.318
Featuring numerous rings of
defensive walls around a central tower,

00:03:59.527 --> 00:04:05.074
this approach created a circular fortress
with multiple layers of siege security.

00:04:05.408 --> 00:04:09.328
From the 13th century onward,
this design reigned supreme,

00:04:09.578 --> 00:04:13.833
even standing up to the small cannons
that had begun spreading across Europe.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:15.835
But by the 15th century,

00:04:15.835 --> 00:04:19.005
the high towers that once rained
arrows on attackers

00:04:19.088 --> 00:04:22.383
became easy targets
for powerful new cannons.

00:04:23.384 --> 00:04:26.637
While castle builders adapted
by making squatter structures

00:04:26.637 --> 00:04:28.639
with walls up to three meters thick,

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:31.809
this kind of construction
was prohibitively expensive.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.145
By the early 16th century,

00:04:34.145 --> 00:04:37.148
most local nobles couldn’t keep
up with the costs,

00:04:37.148 --> 00:04:40.151
and became reliant
on strong centralized rulers

00:04:40.359 --> 00:04:45.031
who could afford to support modern
defenses and large standing armies.

00:04:45.406 --> 00:04:50.745
This financial and political pressure,
alongside advances in gunpowder weapons,

00:04:50.745 --> 00:04:53.622
put an end to castles’ military dominance.

00:04:53.831 --> 00:04:56.834
Most of these fortresses
were converted to residences,

00:04:56.959 --> 00:04:59.462
administrative centers, or prisons,

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:04.258
and today many still serve as tourist
attractions and private estates.

00:05:04.967 --> 00:05:08.804
But while they no longer protect
their inhabitants from sieging armies,

00:05:08.971 --> 00:05:12.183
they still project the grandeur
and authority

00:05:12.224 --> 00:05:16.437
that made medieval castles
so effective for centuries.

